/-!
# A perfect immersion with finite locally free conormal sheaf is regular

**An immersion `i : X ⟶ Y` into a locally Noetherian scheme whose conormal sheaf is finite
locally free and which is perfect is a regular immersion.**  This is Stacks tag 0FK1, and the
source's proof is the translation into geometry of the algebraic criterion of Stacks tag 0FJS.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isRegularImmersion_of_perfect_of_conormalSheaf`:
  **Stacks tag 0FK1**.

## Implementation notes

The source says only "translated into algebra, this is Divided Power Algebra, Proposition 0FJS",
so the content here is exactly that translation, carried out on one affine chart at a time.

Regularity of an immersion is regularity of the defining ideal sheaf of its canonical closed
factor `i.liftCoborder : X ⟶ i.coborderRange`, and the affine criterion of Stacks tag 063G
(`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isRegularIdealSheaf_iff_exists_affine_generators`)
asks for an affine open of `i.coborderRange` around each point of the image carrying generators
of that ideal sheaf which form a weakly regular sequence of sections.  Over an affine open `V` of
`i.coborderRange` the ideal in question is the kernel `I` of `Γ(i.coborderRange, V) ⟶ Γ(X, j⁻¹V)`,
and the three hypotheses of Stacks tag 0FJS are supplied as follows.

* Noetherianity of `Γ(i.coborderRange, V)` is `IsLocallyNoetherian.component_noetherian`, the
  open subscheme of a locally Noetherian scheme being locally Noetherian.
* Finiteness of the projective dimension of `I` comes from perfectness.  The closed factor is
  perfect by flat base change along the open immersion `i.coborderRange.ι` (Stacks tag 0688), so
  `Γ(X, j⁻¹V)` is a perfect `Γ(i.coborderRange, V)`-module; a perfect module has finite
  projective dimension, and the short exact sequence `0 → I → Γ(i.coborderRange, V) → Γ(X, j⁻¹V)
  → 0` with projective middle term transfers that to `I`.
* Finite local freeness of `I/I²` over the quotient comes from the conormal sheaf.  Local
  freeness of a sheaf of modules gives free finite modules of sections over every open contained
  in a member of the trivializing cover, because a finite coproduct of copies of the structure
  sheaf is a finite product and evaluation preserves products.  Choosing `V` inside the image of
  such an open — possible because the closed factor is an embedding — makes the sections of the
  conormal sheaf over `j⁻¹V` free and finite, and the affine computation of the conormal sheaf of
  tag 01R3 identifies them with `I/I²`.

Stacks tag 0FJS concludes that `I` is a regular ideal in the sense of Stacks tag 07CV: at every
prime containing it, its extension to *some* principal localization is generated by a regular
sequence.  The principal localization is the basic open `D(g) ⊆ V`, which is again affine with
`Γ(i.coborderRange, D(g))` the localization away from `g`, and the compatibility of an ideal
sheaf with passing to a basic open identifies the extended ideal with the kernel over `D(g)`.
That is the datum the affine criterion consumes, and the generators are transported from
`Localization.Away g` along the canonical isomorphism.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FK1](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FK1)
